Fix: rescue a deep HIT_DIVERGED prefix hit when the offload tier can back
it but the connector's lookup is structurally blind to the boundary chunk
(vLLM 0.27.1, hybrid Mamba/GDN + full-attention models with mamba-align
offloading).

Root cause (second, smaller structural gap after
patches/offload-eagle-misclassification-mamba.patch's 2026-08-23
revision): OffloadingConnectorScheduler._lookup() starts every group's
scan at start_chunk_idx = num_computed_tokens // tokens_per_chunk --
strictly BEYOND the presented local boundary. A mamba checkpoint sitting
exactly AT that boundary (chunk start_chunk_idx - 1, e.g. when the
previous turn's decode never crossed the next 832-token align boundary)
is invisible. The connector then legitimately answers ext=0 by API
semantics ("N NEW tokens beyond the boundary"), and the core scheduler's
HIT_DIVERGED reconcile -- correct for mamba-state safety when the tier
truly can't back the boundary -- throws away the entire deep
full-attention hit (30-50K tokens observed) and reprefills from the
shallow common boundary, even though the tier held everything needed.

The fix (v1/core/sched/scheduler.py, the
`if hit_diverged and num_external_computed_tokens == 0:` branch): before
reconciling down, re-query the connector ONCE with the presented local
boundary lowered by exactly one mamba-align chunk
(retry_local = round_down(block_aligned_local, align) - align):

  - ext2 > 0: resume at retry_local + ext2 (>= the old deep boundary
    rounded down to the align size; usually >= the old deep boundary,
    since the tier typically also backs the previous turn's decode-tail
    chunk beyond it). The local computed blocks are TRUNCATED to
    retry_local (same truncate_computed_blocks helper the existing
    "remote strictly exceeds local hit" path uses) so the boundary chunk
    is re-loaded from the tier rather than adopted locally -- that is
    what makes the mamba group's boundary chunk loadable at all. Cost:
    one extra attention-chunk DMA (megabytes) instead of a full
    reprefill. Logged as "HIT_DIVERGED RESCUED".
  - ext2 is None (connector deferred: async backend lookup kicked off,
    or the hit chunks are already being loaded): requeue the request for
    the next schedule attempt, exactly like the first lookup's None
    path. Never reconcile-and-recompute a hit the connector may yet
    confirm.
  - ext2 == 0: the tier genuinely can't back the boundary; fall through
    to the existing reconcile ("HIT_DIVERGED RECONCILE") unchanged.

Correctness invariants preserved (each verified against the code):
  1. Never resume at a boundary without a confirmed-loadable mamba chunk
     ending exactly there: the rescue only acts on ext2 > 0, which goes
     through the connector's normal _lookup (per-group confirmed hits,
     including the 1-chunk sliding-window mamba lookup) and
     update_state_after_alloc load-job machinery. update_state_after_
     alloc early-returns on num_external_tokens == 0; the rescue never
     tries to load with ext==0.
  2. get_num_new_matched_tokens is safe to repeat: it clears per-group
     block_ids and re-sets num_locally_computed_tokens at entry, and an
     in-flight transfer would already have made the FIRST call return
     None (transfer_jobs is checked at entry), so the retry cannot race
     a transfer the first call started.
  3. At most ONE retry lookup per schedule attempt (straight-line code,
     no loop); the deferred path requeues via the scheduler's existing
     skipped-waiting mechanism.
  4. Async scheduling: the rescue stays entirely inside the schedule()
     lookup phase; nothing moves across the build_connector_meta()
     boundary.
  5. The align size is taken from the connector
     (OffloadingConnectorScheduler._mamba_align_size via a new
     get_mamba_align_size() accessor, exposed through
     OffloadingConnector; the core scheduler looks it up with getattr,
     so any other connector type simply skips the rescue), and the
     retry boundary is checked for divisibility by the scheduler block
     size before use -- truncate_computed_blocks asserts alignment for
     every group's block size (mamba align = 832 here, a multiple of
     both the mamba block and the attention block).

Evidence for the blind spot: qwen.log.evidence-mamba-free-never-fired.log
events at lines ~5102-5109 / ~27848-27855 (see
docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md 2026-08-23 addendum and
RESULTS-hit-diverged.md). Repro/verification:
test_hit_diverged_rescue.sh, test_offload_regression.sh,
needle_recall_test.py -- results in RESULTS-hit-diverged.md.

